Epicurus

This is a philosopher who helps us think about money, capitalism, and our runaway
consumer societies. Epicurus was an ancient Greek born in 341 BC. What made him
famous was that he spent all his life trying to work out the largest puzzle
there is: what makes people happy? Philosophers before him had discussed at
length what could make people good. Epicurus preferred to look at what's fun.
Unfortunately, the world was bitter and bitchy even then, and when people heard
that Epicurus had set up a school to study happiness the rumours went off the
scale. There were tales that the school hosted ten-course feasts, and orgies
every night. Epicurus was said, by one critic, to have orgasmed eighteen times
in a single evening in a bed full of virgins.

It wasn't true. Epicurus and his team were studying happiness, but they were
doing it very soberly. The philosopher owned only two cloaks, and lived on
bread, olives, and for a treat, an occasional slice of cheese. As for the
bedroom, he merely responded demurely that he'd married philosophy. Having
patiently studied happiness for many years, Epicurus came to a set of remarkable
and revolutionary conclusions about what we actually need to be happy. He
proposed that we typically make three mistakes when thinking about happiness:

Firstly, we think happiness means having romantic, sexual relationships, but
Epicurus looked around and saw so many unhappy couples, their unions marred
by jealousy, misunderstanding, cheating, and bitterness. At the same time, he
observed how much nicer friendships are: how people tend to be so decent and
unpossessive with their friends. Friendship seemed to be where human nature was
at its sweetest. The only problem Epicurus noted was that we don't see our
friends enough.

The next thing we ordinarily think we need to be happy is a lot of money, but
we tend not properly to factor in the unbelievable sacrifices we have to make
to get this money: the jealousy, the backbiting, the long hours. What makes
work really satisfying, Epicurus believed, isn't money, but it's when we're able
to work alone, or in small groups, like in a bakery or a boat repair shop, and
when we feel we're helping others in our own minor way, improving the world.
It isn't really large sums or status that we want deep down. It's a sense of
making a difference.

And lastly, Epicurus observed how obsessive we are with luxury, especially
involving houses and beautiful, serene locations, but beneath our love of
luxury, there is really something else we're trying to get at. What we want
is a feeling of calm. We want our minds pure and free—not full of the normal
bother and chaos. But the great question is: Does luxury actually make us calm?
Epicurus wasn't so sure...

Having looked at happiness in depth, Epicurus announced a revolutionary set of
insights: that you really need only three things to be happy in this life.
Firstly, you need your friends around. No sex, no orgy, just your mates. No more
seeing them only now and then. It's regularity of contact that counts. So
he did that thing that most of us occasionally dream of doing, but never
actually get around to doing. He bought a big house and started living with
all his friends. Everyone had their own quarters and there were pleasant shared
areas too. There's always someone nice to talk to in the kitchen.

Secondly, everyone downshifted. All the members of the commune stopped working
for other people. They took big pay cuts in return for doing their own stuff:
some did farming, some cooking, some pottering or writing. And thirdly, Epicurus
and his friends stopped thinking you could be calm just by having a beautiful
view to look out on. They devoted themselves to finding calm in their own minds:
to spending time on their own, reflecting, writing stuff down, reading things,
meditating. The experiment was so successful, the members of the commune so
happy, the idea spread like wildfire. Epicurean communities opened up all around
the Mediterranean. At the height of the movement, there were four hundred
thousand people living in communes from Spain to Palestine. It was only the
Christian Church that ended things in the fifth century. But they must've
respected the community somehow, because they converted them all into
monasteries. What we know as monasteries are really just Epicurean communes
with a Christian topsoil.

Another interesting fact: Karl Marx did his PhD thesis on Epicurus, and what
we call communism, a gigantic, dull, joyless, failed system, is really a
grown-up, corrupted, not very successful version of Epicureanism. The real
legacy of Epicurus is that human beings aren't very good at making themselves
happy, chiefly because they think it's so easy. We think we know: it's about
sex, money, luxury. We just wonder how to secure all these, but no, says
Epicurus. Reflect on the moments that truly bring you happiness and they aren't
to do with this. Have the courage to change your life, in accordance with
the moments that actually deliver satisfaction. You might end up living in
a very different way: out in the country with just some cheese, a few clothes,
a few philosophy books and some very good friends down the corridor.